.TH BUFFER_GETTOK_SA 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_gettok_sa \- Get a token from a buffer and store it in a stralloc

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>
.IB #include <lib/stralloc.h>

.IB int
.IB buffer_gettok_sa(buffer* b, stralloc* sa, const char* charset, size_t setlen);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_gettok_sa\fR function reads a token from the buffer \fIb\fR and stores it in the stralloc \fIsa\fR. A token is defined as a sequence of characters that are not in the specified charset.

.SH PARAMETERS
.IB b
The buffer from which to read the token.

.IB sa
The stralloc where the token will be stored.

.IB charset
A string containing the characters that define the token.

.IB setlen
The length of the charset string.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The \fBbuffer_gettok_sa\fR function returns the number of characters read on success, or 0 if no characters were read, or -1 if an error occurred (e.g., memory allocation failure).

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_gettok_sa\fR function to read a token from a buffer and store it in a stralloc:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>
#include <lib/stralloc.h>

int main() {
    buffer b;
    stralloc sa;
    const char* charset = " \t\n";
    size_t setlen = 3;

    buffer_init(&b, "Hello, World!");
    stralloc_init(&sa);

    int result = buffer_gettok_sa(&b, &sa, charset, setlen);

    if (result > 0) {
        // Your code to use the token stored in sa here
        printf("Token: %s\n", sa.s);
    } else if (result == 0) {
        printf("No token found\n");
    } else {
        printf("Error: %d\n", errno);
    }

    buffer_done(&b);
    stralloc_done(&sa);

    return 0;
}
.nef